  "answer_text": "If you are looking for **Luka, Mississippi**, you are most likely looking for **Iuka, Mississippi** (spelled with a capital **\"I\"** as in India, which looks like a lowercase **\"l\"** in many digital fonts). \n\nPronounced **\"eye-YOO-kuh\"** (/a\u026a\u02c8ju\u02d0k\u0259/), it is a historic town in the northeastern corner of the state.\n\n---\n\n### **1. Geography & Climate**\n* **Location:** Iuka is the county seat of Tishomingo County, situated near the borders of Alabama and Tennessee.\n* **Woodall Mountain:** Just south of the town lies Woodall Mountain, which is the **highest point in Mississippi** at 806 feet above sea level. \n* **Unique Terrain:** Unlike the flat, low-lying topography that covers most of Mississippi, Iuka sits among the foothills of the Appalachian Mountains in the Tennessee River Valley. This gives the area a hilly, heavily forested landscape and slightly cooler temperatures than the rest of the state.\n\n### **2. History**\n* **Origins:** The town was founded in 1857 along the route of the Memphis and Charleston Railroad. It was built on the site of a Chickasaw Indian village and named after one of its chiefs, **Chief Ish-ta-ki-yu-ka-tubbe**.\n* **The Battle of Iuka (Civil War):** On September 19, 1862, the town was the site of a major American Civil War battle. Union forces under General William S. Rosecrans clashed with Confederate forces under General Sterling Price. \n  * *Acoustic Shadow:* The battle is famous in military history for an \"acoustic shadow.\" Due to wind and atmospheric conditions, Union General Ulysses S. Grant and General Edward Ord\u2014who were only a few miles away awaiting the sound of gunshots to launch a coordinated attack\u2014could not hear the ferocious battle taking place.\n* **Award-Winning Springs:** Iuka is famous for its mineral springs. At the 1904 World\u2019s Fair in St. Louis, water from Iuka's mineral springs won first prize for the \"purest and best mineral water\" in the world. \n\n### **3. Demographics & Economy**\n* **Population:** As of the 2020 census, the population was 3,139.\n* **Key Industries:** Today, the economy relies on local manufacturing, tourism, and recreation. The building of the Pickwick Landing Dam and Pickwick Lake by the Tennessee Valley Authority (TVA) in the 1930s turned the area into a popular destination for boating, fishing, and outdoor recreation.\n\n### **4. Major Attractions**\n* **Tishomingo State Park:** Located just south of Iuka, it is considered one of the most beautiful state parks in Mississippi, featuring massive rock formations, hiking trails, and a historic swinging bridge.\n* **Mineral Springs Park:** Located in downtown Iuka, this park features the historic mineral springs that put the town on the map in the early 20th century. \n* **The Iuka Covered Bridge:** A beautiful, historic pedestrian covered bridge located in town.\n* **Old Courthouse Museum:** Houses a collection of local history, Native American artifacts, and Civil War memorabilia.",
